\description{
\code{instrument_packages()} instruments packages installed in a library, so
that every R process that loads one of them counts the code it runs and
saves the counts in a trace directory when it exits. \code{trace_coverage()}
reads the counts saved there into a single \link{coverage} object, and
\code{uninstrument_packages()} restores the packages. \code{local_instrumented_packages()}
instruments them until the calling function returns.

This is the workflow \code{\link[=package_coverage]{package_coverage()}} uses, split up so that the code
can be run by anything: the tests of several packages, a test runner or
build system, or a script that starts many R processes. The traces of
several packages, and of processes that load more than one of them, are
kept apart, so one directory can collect the coverage of a set of packages,
e.g. those of a monorepo.
}
\section{Requirements}{

\itemize{
\item The packages must be installed with their source references, e.g. with
\verb{R CMD INSTALL --with-keep.source}. File names in the coverage are those
of the source the packages were installed from.
\item covr must be installed in a library that the R processes use, as the
instrumented packages call it when they are loaded, unless they were
instrumented with \code{trace_dir = NULL} and \code{COVR_TRACE_DIR} is not set:
they then load as installed, without covr.
\item Processes must exit normally to save their counts; processes that are
killed lose them, and \code{trace_coverage()} warns (with class
\code{covr_unsaved_trace}) about the processes that loaded an instrumented
package but didn't save their counts.
}

The R processes save their counts in the directory in the environment
variable \code{COVR_TRACE_DIR}, or if it is not set, in \code{trace_dir}. Packages
instrumented with \code{trace_dir = NULL} are only measured when
\code{COVR_TRACE_DIR} is set, and otherwise load the code they were installed
with, without covr, so they can be instrumented once, e.g. when they are
built, and their coverage measured whenever it is set. Compiled code isn't
instrumented.

The coverage includes the instrumented packages that no process loaded,
with none of their code covered, if they were instrumented with a
\code{trace_dir}, or if \code{trace_coverage()} is given their \code{lib}. This needs
them to be instrumented when they are installed, which works for most
packages (see the value of \code{instrument_packages()}).

The options that change what is measured, such as
\link{covr.branch_coverage} and \link{covr.record_tests}, and
\code{covr.fix_parallel_mcexit} (see \code{\link[=package_coverage]{package_coverage()}}), are read when
the packages are instrumented. Unlike \code{\link[=package_coverage]{package_coverage()}},
\code{instrument_packages()} doesn't set the environment variable \code{R_COVR}, so
\code{\link[=in_covr]{in_covr()}} is \code{FALSE} in the processes unless they set it.
}

